APPENDIX B — h_R[x]  (CLASSICAL RELATIONAL FORM)
============================================================
  The relational horizon h_R[x] is the set of elements that are
  maximally reachable from x under the transitive closure R*.
  It captures the boundary of relational reachability in any
  structure defined by a binary relation.

SETUP
-----
  X        = underlying set
  R ⊆ X×X  = binary relation
  R*       = transitive closure of R

RELATIONAL NEIGHBORHOOD
-----------------------
  R[x] := { y ∈ X | (x,y) ∈ R }

REACHABLE SET
-------------
  Reach[x] := { y ∈ X | (x,y) ∈ R* }

RELATIONAL HORIZON (CLASSICAL)
------------------------------
  h_R[x] := { y ∈ X |
                (x,y) ∈ R*       and
                there is no z ∈ X with (y,z) ∈ R* }

Interpretation:
  - Compute all points reachable from x via chains of R.
  - Keep only those that cannot reach any new points.
  - These are the maximal reachable elements under R*.
  - This forms the relational horizon of x.

EXAMPLE
-------
  X = {1,2,3,4,5}
  R = {(1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(4,5)}

  R* = {(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),
        (2,3),(2,4),(2,5),
        (3,4),(3,5),
        (4,5)}

  h_R[1] = {5}

  5 is the maximal reachable element from 1.

GEOMETRIC INTERPRETATION
------------------------
  - R defines adjacency or visibility.
  - R* defines all reachable points.
  - h_R[x] is the boundary of the reachable region.
  - This generalizes geometric horizons to arbitrary relations.
